#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128948 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d4c1a7b-2341-5b8c-8112-0290f8eda65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Block Diagonalization of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "1cc03cef-e717-5ac1-ab9d-75508f3aa056" :question/math-academy-id "e-5533" :question/problem "![](images/70/7014ce766be779f4231c78a99a6e013bfc48bfd9e21da7fa565c49e466437c99.png)\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2 \\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PBP^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](images/5e/5e03c481312af462a0739b4670e24325d2e06cc10449c65bfb04208206fc5d58.png)\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )^{2}-1(-1)) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n$\\lambda_1={\\color{red}-1}.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{(4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 5}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 2\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n![](images/71/712c3f663a367090ab5ba81705e7c7f36ce6d4e2d6e2b13cc59383421e408ea7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128949 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a65788-ff15-58e9-8329-c69b2a8e311b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "fb7c97e6-f106-5a47-ad01-75205c0d2603" :question/math-academy-id "e-5534" :question/problem "Consider the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2 \\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n![](images/31/31a6051588cdb215a025179138803257d01f72cb2f231cda05cbbd67b56b0fb9.png)\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=2$ and $\\lambda_2=1-\\textrm{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1},$ find the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](images/2a/2a08ff9b84de36c2b9141809f385f688f6b8c83b2b916bd3469432705636a154.png)\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n![](images/50/50b840579f0396b87913b773975c26b337c9846c139bc4a1bf9445984681f062.png)\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${{\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}}},$ and the second and third columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} .$ Therefore, \n\n![](images/55/55b4ecb56daf7f8cce19878af9f17e237a5f38b1593a4000d39487cb93ff2c95.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128950 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129139 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ccd12c-a814-5ebb-985c-eef8707001f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of an NxN Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "2b2a7fbc-8911-507f-915c-cf9b50653e5f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5535" :question/problem "Consider the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2 \\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n![](images/5f/5fd08491c00587e0efa4017850407c58af8ad0821c2c1aab512ec20b5295d128.png)\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=7 + 4\\textrm{i}$ and $\\lambda_3=-3 + 2\\textrm{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1},$ find a possible matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\qquad$ ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}=\\overline{\\color{red}\\lambda}_{\\color{red}2} \\quad$ and $\\quad {\\color{blue}\\lambda_3}=\\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\qquad$ ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} = \\overline{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{red}2} \\quad$ and $\\quad {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}4},$ \n\nthen $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](images/c8/c8b3063b226f25c53f064e3f427446da7cc4c7cd99a20b8c1d9e46d33bd429e0.png)\nand\n![](images/04/0489fb7ddf3f73682e4e61345fa73a601c5f8ae01bf319918becd9c2426cc73d.png)\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n![](images/35/350a5319ff61e3f794e780561616c7a9523f18832ee040ce8faa0825818f8f24.png)\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3}.$ Therefore,\n![](images/c6/c6213ccfec2acbc449e38e431ef1d60d2e06b19066b735faa6426dfd4b326c9c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128978 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6704692a-2219-559f-9352-97c5fb590894" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Block Diagonal Form"}] :question/id #uuid "da515ea7-f4b0-58c5-adab-c826563dd3cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5906" :question/problem "![](images/13/139905ee461ed3a946aa232e42e65f619f1550884b24dc72354303e3af7a05df.png)\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2 \\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](images/66/66747ae8628c72623aa51610ed39af91154d06d61e67189f93d04079b64f494a.png)\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n![](images/ee/eef90c4d866110a74a221ce82ad275de5b07ddbf6e33213d94cf5f7831295022.png)\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_{1,2}=1 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\color{red}\\lambda_3=-3}.$\n\n- From our matrix \n![](images/54/5449e99fabebae1514f40bdf40698e84bb60a578e495e5a363ed7ac617749ad2.png)\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n![](images/cd/cdf7fb1cb310f63e0dcf468aa045dc0fb1bb3b2e757ae431ae499c898ab5538f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6704692a-2219-559f-9352-97c5fb590894" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Block Diagonal Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe060a57-c5fa-5e13-816e-242c1ae0f810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400718} {:answer/id #uuid "69365ed6-d82e-5f46-8caf-0e16ac9d1e61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =4\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5∓4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400719} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c8a5e0-33df-5e97-ae29-be024ad7b1e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400720} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed7cbfd-bc73-59a1-9f19-e8c4e4e1fb8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm \\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1∓4\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400721} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4204d9-9e4e-5bbe-b5eb-e89b156fba7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7c8a5e0-33df-5e97-ae29-be024ad7b1e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400720} :answer-field/id #uuid "717a76be-6f3b-501d-a9ed-63a7517d5688" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db2db2cc-8132-5f94-95c8-6b73da358424" :question/math-academy-id "q-135440" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{2,3}=3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$\n\n- From our matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n{v}_{2,3}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6704692a-2219-559f-9352-97c5fb590894" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Block Diagonal Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b5a9d49-25f9-5366-8bc7-c890c3a78895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-5\\pm 7\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-4∓6\\text{i} \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266527} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf84c1b-3625-51fc-8524-a1d7940f3c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-5\\pm \\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ ∓\\text{i} \\\\ 3∓5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267098} {:answer/id #uuid "71f304ba-84ea-5528-bcb3-689937cb3050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =7\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267143} {:answer/id #uuid "9e57d696-3ab4-5b6f-bbfb-0232c8d15f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =5\\pm 7\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267570} {:answer/id #uuid "b500b7f7-d867-52f2-935d-95ccf584db7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =5\\pm \\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5∓3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e57d696-3ab4-5b6f-bbfb-0232c8d15f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =5\\pm 7\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267570} :answer-field/id #uuid "88d98f8c-5de6-52d5-9b57-7724dc9a6529" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f4184b0-1aa9-527d-b4bd-2a5a8359efb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-135441" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{2,3}=5\\pm 7\\text{i}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$\n\n- From our matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n{v}_{2,3}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6704692a-2219-559f-9352-97c5fb590894" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Block Diagonal Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11504871-6e11-558c-a4a9-c918ea05ff9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =8\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ ∓7\\text{i} \\\\ 5∓3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266288} {:answer/id #uuid "1797afb6-8aac-566a-b295-c8e35e784155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm 8\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1∓4\\text{i} \\\\ ∓7\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266339} {:answer/id #uuid "87106873-8a44-5d40-96ea-a6501b636344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =5\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ ∓7\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267337} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b283eb-f9f6-57ab-94e6-2c5847ed233b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =8\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ ∓7\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267935} {:answer/id #uuid "fc97233b-eafb-51b3-ba25-8ec19eca0bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-8\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm 7\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7b283eb-f9f6-57ab-94e6-2c5847ed233b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =8\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ ∓7\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267935} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0a4d8c3-34a1-52c4-b97c-5b869de2f209" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bef37eb1-45d3-5a03-bda7-0732ce91a120" :question/math-academy-id "q-135467" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=8\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$\n\n- From our matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n{v}_{1,2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ ∓7\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d4c1a7b-2341-5b8c-8112-0290f8eda65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Block Diagonalization of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b8c81ff-4fba-5662-9d08-3e487f024917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266814} {:answer/id #uuid "670db349-289c-59c6-92ff-3b70af48ecc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267059} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed5f3c0-e1f4-5079-9eb6-011ae7b51c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267576} {:answer/id #uuid "d0cd01a2-51bc-5011-b65e-f6c8137a55cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268010} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ee0fa6-ec3c-5515-be0f-532aac7019ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ed5f3c0-e1f4-5079-9eb6-011ae7b51c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267576} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b631103-d455-5323-9f2f-94279388a60e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24acf1d5-f36a-5ae6-986a-efa25c9f1966" :question/math-academy-id "q-135534" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 4-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )^{2}-5(-5)) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +29) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{(4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 29}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{-100}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 10\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129139 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ccd12c-a814-5ebb-985c-eef8707001f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of an NxN Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f74e1b7-608b-52c3-a126-6431a8a49060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266716} {:answer/id #uuid "4517f571-aeef-5aa8-aa64-17dbc03ef85c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266760} {:answer/id #uuid "687dcc62-5d5b-560d-bc4c-c49b6e8a1348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267072} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c540bb-c4e7-544b-adec-6d73170048d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267866} {:answer/id #uuid "ea70bd41-1595-5628-9b4d-52a042c12081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "687dcc62-5d5b-560d-bc4c-c49b6e8a1348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267072} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7442b78-1ddf-5101-a0db-8527d16d2d96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a7fef9f6-d241-50d5-bf0b-c6bab4ef198e" :question/math-academy-id "q-135537" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2\\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3+\\text{i} \\\\ 5-2\\text{i} \\\\ 2+4\\text{i} \\\\ 3-4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4-7\\text{i} \\\\ 1-2\\text{i} \\\\ 3+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5+\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129139 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ccd12c-a814-5ebb-985c-eef8707001f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of an NxN Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81edab10-6956-528b-91cb-f9fb1e627150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -7 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388391} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a94d76-71ed-5a8e-981f-0131b6dc27f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -7 & 0 & -7 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 2 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388392} {:answer/id #uuid "7a2c8fa7-7d76-5149-a9f4-9a3fa3c87f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -7 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388393} {:answer/id #uuid "93ad9fb5-d339-5115-9174-e3d917773018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -7 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388394} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5ee829-5867-5f7d-8705-8aa5c9396db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -7 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93ad9fb5-d339-5115-9174-e3d917773018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -7 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388394} :answer-field/id #uuid "a66467bc-9a50-5d8e-837a-eeefeb49dc48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "665587c5-1a3a-55cd-89d1-5eea160afc62" :question/math-academy-id "q-135540" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2\\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7+2\\text{i} \\\\ -2-7\\text{i} \\\\ -2-2\\text{i} \\\\ -3-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}5-5\\text{i} \\\\ -7 \\\\ -2+3\\text{i} \\\\ -5-5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2+\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -7 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a65788-ff15-58e9-8329-c69b2a8e311b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b219e25-b7de-54f7-986a-38c98e277a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388432} {:answer/id #uuid "d200c247-9d3b-5fa3-bd48-0ef09f7a0d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388433} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed58743-d23a-5a94-83d7-2860fff3387f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388434} {:answer/id #uuid "d59e6ebf-0843-5ef4-8fe1-d5fbc89f05aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388435} {:answer/id #uuid "829a0c6b-0865-5fd5-a46b-8e4ebd523c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ed58743-d23a-5a94-83d7-2860fff3387f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388434} :answer-field/id #uuid "9afe0ea9-8480-5156-92b1-0524f8f503e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8670ee42-0198-5e90-b231-dedde453ca87" :question/math-academy-id "q-135643" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3+5\\text{i} \\\\ 1-6\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+8\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-5,$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{3},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & {v}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a65788-ff15-58e9-8329-c69b2a8e311b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "175ad214-847b-57ec-ba0a-35665534ee96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266337} {:answer/id #uuid "1f2d55e5-f79a-542a-ba87-67fb08e50bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266416} {:answer/id #uuid "48e6699e-ac33-5ea9-b694-64ef66b3dbfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266796} {:answer/id #uuid "6adba04a-cab3-592c-9a8a-b48189435b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267090} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d056e9-1d8b-57ba-a405-6ee3b9e35e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2d056e9-1d8b-57ba-a405-6ee3b9e35e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268301} :answer-field/id #uuid "030bc847-8b2b-53be-9a9c-267a88e0fad3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2473b775-36eb-5e2f-9d42-ae0339f64aa2" :question/math-academy-id "q-135660" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4-\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\text{i} \\\\ 3-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+6\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1,$  respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{3},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & {v}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a65788-ff15-58e9-8329-c69b2a8e311b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "808df640-83cd-5e1a-8acb-95e037dec902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388439} {:answer/id #uuid "73a0e2dd-c0da-58bf-abf3-c3f1b1aeba17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388440} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb8eb39-67ad-536a-bab6-c0527609c5d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388441} {:answer/id #uuid "aac71ff1-edc8-50b4-a7f3-1638b059fb25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388442} {:answer/id #uuid "21727534-854f-594d-946c-4a829dc9fd8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "808df640-83cd-5e1a-8acb-95e037dec902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388439} :answer-field/id #uuid "64ccbbe4-08d2-5598-989a-927014c477e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2aef9402-e7a5-51fc-b3a5-92703ff2805a" :question/math-academy-id "q-135662" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ -6+3\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4+8\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ and the second and third columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a65788-ff15-58e9-8329-c69b2a8e311b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d159a3-8f7f-5702-9187-3f67068b60a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324013} {:answer/id #uuid "728999fd-5749-5cee-a90e-8647dd1c1b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324922} {:answer/id #uuid "a8842f3e-06fd-5dd8-aee7-73b4f4366e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325406} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0c940a-9cc0-58ac-9e7a-d4fcacea51e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325427} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd2b8b8-b3db-5d04-82d4-deb11ee2260a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8842f3e-06fd-5dd8-aee7-73b4f4366e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325406} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ec31f8-d7dd-5c84-9833-21e5dc79c7e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97813176-8f27-5690-98e4-6d6c62e9f0d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-38660" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5+7\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i} \\\\ \\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1-2\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2,$  respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{3},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & {v}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6704692a-2219-559f-9352-97c5fb590894" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Block Diagonal Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "304a0e30-d1e1-5799-a18b-3f81288646d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =5\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i} \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 1∓\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324356} {:answer/id #uuid "467a7464-9959-5d31-a641-556fd5ae4853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 1∓\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324539} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec9c86b-cafa-5790-93c0-70a53b6c15e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 1∓\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325316} {:answer/id #uuid "e304faf9-90df-5cb4-96c8-bb7f0c303559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ ∓\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325965} {:answer/id #uuid "e311d8eb-2223-5f22-8ba8-763f362de354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =5\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ \\pm 2\\text{ } \\\\ 1∓\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ec9c86b-cafa-5790-93c0-70a53b6c15e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 1∓\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325316} :answer-field/id #uuid "96c35a60-8006-5ec6-9c7e-ca1861d7466b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "582f937c-6cfb-53b4-966a-6cea9b2086e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-38665" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{2,3}=-2\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$\n\n- From our matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n{v}_{2,3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 1∓\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129139 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ccd12c-a814-5ebb-985c-eef8707001f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of an NxN Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bec3827-231c-556a-a0b7-54f8d9348b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324456} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4f90f2-dfb3-5853-86f9-c1ac39d8f5e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324583} {:answer/id #uuid "7a139465-9f74-545d-896e-f96a9b5d526b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324991} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7de5fe-c21c-5372-8be7-65c7a015e53a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325029} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ecf648-b192-5e75-aed6-35c76e8b200a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a139465-9f74-545d-896e-f96a9b5d526b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324991} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e7e2791-b8eb-5d85-a01f-905df068b3d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3453f8e0-6d91-5e5f-9160-30bae2ecd536" :question/math-academy-id "q-38921" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2\\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1+\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6-2\\text{i} \\\\ 2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3-2\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1-5\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}]}
